<html>
<title>Directory and File Structure</title>
<body>
<h1>Directory and File Structure</h1>

<p>
After initial startup of Emma 02, a default directory and file structure will be created as described in the subsections below. These files can be reinstalled at any time using the <strong>Menu Item</strong> below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/menu_reinstall.png" border="0" width="395" height="128">
</p>

<p>
The default data directory location is:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Windows Vista and later: C:\Users\<em>username</em>\AppData\Roaming\Emma 02</li>
<li>Windows XP: C:\Documents and Settings\<em>username</em>\Application Data\Emma 02</li>
<li>macOS: ~/Emma 02</li>
<li>Linux: ~/emma_02_data</li>
</ul>

<p>
If an older version of Emma 02 was used previously, the data directory might instead be located here:
</p>

<ul>
<li>macOS: ~/Documents/emma_02</li>
<li>Linux: ~/Documents/emma_02</li>
</ul>

<p>
To change the location of the data directory, use the <strong>Menu Item</strong> below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/menu_change_data_dir.png" border="0" width="247" height="128">
</p>

<p>
A window will open containing the following buttons:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Browse</strong>: Select a new location</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Save</strong>: Save the new location</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Move & Save</strong>: Save and move the existing data directory and files to the new location</li>
</ul>

<p>
If the <strong>Save</strong> option is used, the data directory must be moved manually by the user.
</p>

<p>
When moving the data directory to another disk, the process may take a few minutes depending on disk speed.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Important:</strong> In all cases, emulator settings will be reset to their default values (similar to the <strong>Set Default (All)</strong> menu action).
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/menu_change_data_dir_window.png" border="0" width="425" height="230">
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> When installing a new version of Emma 02, the data directory is NOT reinstalled automatically to avoid overwriting user changes. To install the latest files from the Emma 02 installation package, use the re-install option shown at the top of this page.
</p>


<h2>CDP18S020</h2>
<p>
Directory containing the <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cdp18s020.html">CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit</a>, including the UT4 ROM and Tiny Basic.
</p>


<h2>Chip 8</h2>
<p>
Directory containing Chip-8, Chip-8X, Chip-10, and Super-chip software. This software can be used with COSMAC VIP, TMC 2000, Nano, Studio II, Studio III, Conic, and MPT-02, and where supported, also on ETI660 and Studio IV.
</p>

<p>
Subdirectories:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Chip-8 Demos</li>
<li>Chip-8 ETI660 Hybrids (ETI660 only)</li>
<li>Chip-8 Games</li>
<li>Chip-8 Hires (extended resolution interpreter)</li>
<li>Chip-8 Hybrids (CDP1802 + Chip-8)</li>
<li>Chip-8 Programs</li>
<li>Chip-8X</li>
<li>Chip-10</li>
<li>Super-chip Games (see the Emma 02 website <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/pseudo_super_chip.html">Super-chip</a> section)</li>
</ul>


<h2>Cidelsa</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cidelsa.html">Cidelsa</a> ROMs and CrossLib games such as <code>Xbomber</code>, <code>Xchase</code> and <code>Xshoot</code>.
</p>


<h2>CoinArcade</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/coin_arcade.html">RCA Video Coin Arcade</a> ROMs.
</p>


<h2>Comx</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/comx.html">COMX-35</a> system and expansion ROM files.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Demo</h3>
<p>
F&amp;M demos that were not released as full standalone games.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Disks</h3>
<p>
Disk images including COMX DOS 1.4, F&amp;M Disk Tools, and various software packages. Some software (e.g., <code>Hit It</code>, <code>Comxstar</code>) is only available on disk.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Educatief</h3>
<p>
Dutch educational programs.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Educational</h3>
<p>
English educational programs.
</p>


<h3>Comx/F&amp;M Basic</h3>
<p>
Example programs for F&amp;M Basic.
</p>


<h3>Comx/F&amp;M EPROM Switchboard</h3>
<p>
Firmware for the F&amp;M EPROM Switchboard.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Games</h3>
<p>
English games.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Hulpmiddelen - Utilities</h3>
<p>
Utility software.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Plotter</h3>
<p>
Software for the COMX PL-80 plotter. Some programs require disk images (see Comx/Disks).
</p>


<h3>Comx/Programmas</h3>
<p>
Dutch programs.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Programs</h3>
<p>
English programs.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Spellen</h3>
<p>
Dutch games.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Talen - Languages</h3>
<p>
Programming languages.
</p>


<h3>Comx/Usb FW</h3>
<p>
COMX Super Board firmware files. See the <a href="https://www.comx35.com/superboard.html">COMX Super Board</a> website.
</p>


<h2>Conic</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/victory.html">RCA Studio III / Conic</a> ROM files, multi-cart ROM, Studio II Chip-8 and some cartridge files (with st2 extension) for some additional game ROMs.
</p>


<h2>CosmacGameSystem</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/rca_game_system.html">RCA COSMAC Computer Game System</a> ROMs.
</p>


<h2>Cosmicos</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cosmicos.html">Cosmicos</a> software, including the ASCII monitor, hex and UT4 monitor, hex monitor, hexmops and mops.
</p>


<h2>Cybervision</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cybervision.html">CyberVision 2001</a> firmware.
</p>


<h2>Elf</h2>
<p>
Directory containing Elf software, including Elf/Os, Giant Board Monitor, Fig Forth and <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/basic_quest_6_0.html">Quest Super Basic</a>.
</p>


<h3>Biosio</h3>
<p>
BIOSIO files for Elf/Os 0.2.8.
</p>


<h3>Biosio PS2</h3>
<p>
BIOSIO PS2 files for Elf/Os 0.2.8.
</p>


<h3>Elf/elf-emulation.com</h3>
<p>
Software from the <a href="https://github.com/rileym65/Website-ElfEmulation">Elf/1802 Emulation</a> website, including source and documentation. All software in this directory is copyright by Michael H Riley.
</p>

<p>
To run this software, use <code>bios1.0.4.rom</code> as main ROM with VT100 terminal emulation and start or auto boot hex address as listed:
</p>

<pre>
Dice Rolling.hex           0000
Extended ETOPS.hex         0000
RcForth v1.0.hex           0000
RcForth Rom Version.hex    E000
RcLisp Rom Version.hex     D000
EdtAsm                     C000
EdtAsm Rom Version         8000
Bascom Tiny Basic Compiler 8600
RcBasic L1                 8000
RcBasic L2                 8000
</pre>


<h3>Elf/Games</h3>
<p>
Directory containing several Elf games. See the Configuration section of <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf.html">COSMAC Elf</a> for instructions on loading the Game Mode configuration.
</p>


<h3>Elf/Music</h3>
<p>
Directory containing Elf music software and Christmas songs. See the Configuration section of <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf.html">COSMAC Elf</a> for instructions on loading the Music Machine configuration.
</p>


<h3>Elf/Netronics Tiny Basic</h3>
<p>
Directory containing Netronics Tiny Basic assembler code and manual.
</p>


<h3>Elf/Pico Elf</h3>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/picoelf.html">Pico/Elf V2</a> system ROMs, as well as command and LBR files.
</p>


<h3>Elf/Pico Elf/TMS9918</h3>
<p>
Directory containing multiple TMS9918 examples written by Glenn Jolly. All examples must be loaded and started at address 2000 (hexadecimal).
</p>


<h3>Elf/QuestData</h3>
<p>
Directory containing example code from Quest Data.
</p>


<h3>Elf/SuperBasic</h3>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/basic_quest_6_0.html">Quest Super Basic</a> example software.
</p>


<h3>Elf/TinyBasic</h3>
<p>
Directory containing Tiny Basic example software.
</p>


<h3>Elf/TinyosTS</h3>
<p>
Directory containing Netronics Tiny Basic for the Elf/Os operating system.
</p>


<h3>Elf/VDU</h3>
<p>
Software supporting the ACE VDU board. Two configurations are provided: <strong>VDU / ASCII keyboard</strong> and <strong>VDU / Hex keypad</strong>. The first is for Tiny Basic and Monitor, the second for games. See the Configuration section of <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf.html">COSMAC Elf</a> for instructions on loading these configurations.
</p>


<h2>Elf2K</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf2000.html">COSMAC Elf 2000</a> firmware and software.
</p>


<h2>Eti</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/eti.html">ETI 660</a> firmware and software.
</p>


<h3>Fig Forth CDP1802</h3>
<p>
Fig Forth assembler, listing and hex files.
</p>


<h2>FREDI</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/fred1.html">FRED I</a> software.
</p>


<h2>FREDII</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/fred1_5.html">FRED II</a> software.
</p>


<h2>FREDIII</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/fred3.html">FRED III</a> software.
</p>


<h2>HEC1802</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/eti.html">HEC1802</a> firmware and software.
</p>


<h2>HUG1802</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/eti.html">HUG1802</a> firmware and software.
</p>


<h2>Macbug</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/macbug.html">Macbug</a> firmware.
</p>


<h2>MCDS</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/mcds.html">MCDS</a> firmware, UT62, BASIC3, and EDIT_ASM.
</p>


<h2>Membership</h2>
<p>
Directory containing <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/member.html">Membership Card</a> software, including the Program3 example from the manual.
